# CP12.2 — three loose ends, then charts.
#
# F13. plink_TWrboR36RZ13fH cancelled via POST /v1/payment_links/{id}/cancel.
#      It was created for case_000001 when a second link for case_000000 was
#      refused, then never paid, and it stayed live after the ngrok URL went
#      back to the user's Django app. A payment on it would have sent Razorpay
#      into a 24-hour retry loop against a 404 and then disabled the webhook.
#      Nothing live remains on the account: one paid link, one cancelled.
#
# F14. tests/test_webhook.py's "No TestClient" docstring said httpx was unpinned
#      after CP12.1's F9 pinned it. The decision was right and the stated reason
#      had stopped being true, which made a preference read as a constraint. A
#      comment that contradicts the code is the failure class this project is
#      about, so it is fixed rather than carried. Discharges the BLOCKED note in
#      checkpoints/cp12_1.allowlist.
#
# F15. Known Limitations opened in README, with the Razorpay edge's entries.
#
# No code changed. No test changed. Docstring, docs, and one API call.

tests/test_webhook.py
README.md
SPEC.md
PLAN.md
checkpoints/cp12_2.allowlist

# --- One thing found while doing F15 ---------------------------------------
#
# "Known Limitations" is referenced six times across SPEC.md and PRIORS.md and
# the section had never been written. Every one of those references has been
# pointing at nothing since CP2:
#
#   SPEC §5.3   partial / reduced debit amounts out of scope
#   SPEC §5.3   email out of scope as a channel
#   SPEC §12    G9 notice-as-full-contact, vs. regulatory overhead exempt from G2
#   SPEC §12    G11's TRAI transactional-message exemption, ASSERTED
#   SPEC A98    the G1 / TRAI time-band ambiguity
#   PRIORS:106  one row that could not be verified
#
# The section is opened here with the four Razorpay entries F15 asked for, and
# it states in its own first paragraph that it is incomplete and names all six
# outstanding items. Writing them properly is D5 work and needs SPEC §12 and
# PRIORS open, which this allowlist does not permit. The alternative — a Known
# Limitations section that reads as complete while six entries are missing — is
# worse than not having one, because a reader takes the absence of an entry as
# evidence the limitation does not exist.
